00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
vladimir putin has issued a warning to  the west  insisting that any country that meddles  in russia's affairs  would live to regret their actions  putin's main political opponent alexei  navalny  who survived novichok poisoning last  year has been detained once again  on trumped-up charges he has just ended  a hunger strike of 24 days  russia had also recently deployed more  than 100 000 troops to the border with  ukraine  russia now says it will pull them back  but  it was a worrying reminder of 2014  when russia invaded and annexed crimea  what will be the impact of these actions  and how will the world respond  our experts answer your questions  what does navalny represent for russia  navalny since his return has gained  not just the statue as a politician he  basically moved from  reality from the realm of uh everyday  sort of politics  uh into the stuff of mythology and  fables  his captured imagination of the world  the uh  most famous actors writers poets are  signing  letters and on his behalf  demanding that he is allowed medical  care he has captured the imagination of  of young people both in russia  and outside how should america be  responding to what's going on  in russia  the u.s and other western countries are  having to think through potential  consequences not just  for navalny's treatment but also for a  host of other situations  in the past couple of weeks we have seen  for example a major military buildup on  ukraine's borders and so there are  potential consequences  for that we have seen uh the czech  republic  point to uh or blame russian military  intelligence  for an explosion that took place on that  country in 2014  and america of course in the past uh two  or three weeks  has also expelled russian diplomats it  has imposed sanctions on russia  for cyber activity a major cyber  espionage campaign  known as solar winds and all manner of  other kinds of russian  um policy and so i think the challenge  in thinking of consequences  is not only how do you send a signal  how do you calibrate these consequences  so for example if you use  all your sanctions now in response to  navalny what do you have left in reserve  if there is a russian  attack in eastern ukraine for instance  and i think the other challenge is  at least for the united states is that  although they want to send a tough  message they also want to hold out the  possibility  of dialogue i think that's the american  approach to this they don't want to  simply  squeeze russia or or impose a  punishing swinging costs on the regime  without leaving anything left for future  actions  do sanctions actually work if not  what other options are there i mean they  have worked in some ways whether they've  achieved what they were meant to achieve  as a different question  they worked in uh  certainly imposing a cost on russian  economy  making it more isolated  contributing to stagnation  whether they've deterred russia or if  they changed putin's behavior  i don't think they have at all  russia has shifted to different economic  model  after sanctions were imposed it became  much more of a sort of a siege  economy where the emphasis is on  protecting it from external shocks and  building reserves  on reducing debt uh rather than on  growth  so sanctions certainly have heard uh  but they to to my mind they were always  about  the west has to do something so let's do  that because we don't really have an  alternative but maybe shashank does  uh know sort of other more innovative  ways  i think one interesting thing we see in  the cyber domain  in response to russian cyber activity  whether that's espionage or disruptive  and destructive activity  in cyberspace has been naming and  shaming uh that is  um countries like the us the uk  and other europeans publicly laying out  the evidence  about what russian intelligence agencies  are doing in cyberspace  in ways that they never would have done  10 or 15 years ago they name and shame  not just intelligence agencies but  individual russian hackers  working for the svr or the gru or the  fsb  different russian intelligence agencies  and so that's a shift from big  punishing economic sanctions to targeted  individual  sanctions often against individual  intelligence officers or hackers  in ways that expose their activity  embarrass them and constrain their  behavior whether it has had  a substantial effect on russian behavior  perhaps that's too early to say  but i think it is shows it is indicative  of the evolving  apparatus of pressure on russia that the  west is developing  what was putin's aim in building up  troops on the ukrainian border  well between march and april russia  built up over a hundred thousand troops  on the border with ukraine  and in crimea to the south on april 22nd  russia's defense minister said  the routine exercise as he called it was  complete  and all the troops would be going home  as planned  now it wasn't a routine exercise we know  that this was the biggest buildup of  russian forces  on eastern ukraine and in crimea since  2014 which  is the year of course that russia  invaded ukraine in annexed  crimea so this was not routine this was  a very unusual  even an extraordinary build-up of forces  on a very large  and substantial scale matched by some  very  menacing rhetoric coming out of russia  what was the point of it all  well it's hard to say even western  intelligence agencies i think struggled  to understand  russian motivations and that was the  point russia was keeping its options  open  i think part of this was about russia's  domestic circumstances it was embattled  besieged at home with navalny in the  protests  it may also have been about a new  american president coming into office  joe biden changing american policy on  russia  in a much more robust direction so this  may have been a test  for the white house but above all i  think this was really an effort  to intimidate ukraine so this was  probably  an act of what what what the experts  call coercive diplomacy  muscle flexing intimidation against  ukraine in the hope of extracting  concessions  if that was the purpose it didn't  succeed we didn't actually see  any tangible concessions handed over  from ukraine to russia  and russia has returned home sent its  troops back to barracks  with a summit promised by joe biden but  very little else to show for it  what would happen if russia tries to  annex more of ukraine in the future  i think the response will be very severe  it looks for now as though the worst of  the military crisis  is over if we do indeed see russian  troops moving away from the border and  that is yet to be  uh yet to be evidenced we'll have to  wait and see if that does happen  i think the risk of a russian offensive  against ukraine has diminished  substantially  but if there was a substantial  escalation in the donbass if we did see  a russian effort to increase the  pressure there or if russia were to keep  its troops in place and to keep that  military threat  against ukraine and to use those forces  there's a limit to how far the west  is willing to support ukraine ukraine is  not a member of the nato alliance  but on the other hand there is a  recognition  that a a change in the status quo an  attack from crimea  an attack in eastern ukraine would  represent the biggest  crisis in european security for many  years and i think we would undoubtedly  see  a fresh round of sanctions against  russia it would it would prompt  a much tighter relationship between  ukraine and the west  and a pretty strong economic diplomatic  and even military response from from  europe and america  i'm shashank joshi defense editor at the  economist if you'd like to read more of  our russia coverage please click on the  link  thanks for watching and don't forget to  [Music]  subscribe
 

